But that does not help much to correctly estimate the probabilities.
Even if you got two thirds of your *healing potions from defensive item
generation, their probability of occurrence could still be near to 11 %.
 It just depends on how frequently other potions are generated on
monsters.  As I discussed in my answer to Marc, the increased
probability of potions of object detection dropped by monsters has an
influence on the probability of potions of *healing dropped by monsters,
decreasing it.  All that one could say from a statement that about half
of the *healing potions obtained are from other sources than the floor
would be that this does not increase the probability in question to more
than 22 %. However, as you do get *other* potions from monsters as well
(speed, gain level, etc.), the increase will be lower than this.  But I
agree that the real probability could be well close to 15 % or so.
Nevertheless, I will refrain from doing a calculation with this new
estimate, as I have no idea how reliable it is.

Instead let us just wait for the outcome of Derek's measurements.  What
is needed to do a meaningful calculations is two numbers only: the
probability (obtained by averaging over a sufficient number of games)
that a potion in possession of the player is a potion of *healing and
the average number of potions per level collected by the player.  One
could, of course, measure additional interesting quantities.  The
variance, for example, of the probability distribution to confirm the
nature of the binomial distribution.  Moreover, I would find it most
interesting to record the mentioned probability and average as a
function of level reached to see how it changes with the length of a
game.  This would require a larger sample of games to get precise
estimates. (In addition, one could record the numbers of potions
generated, not just the numbers collected or picked up, for comparison.)

Anyway, what is *not* necessary, is a detailed consideration of all the
possible sources from which you get or don't get potions or of different
gameplay, for that matter.  That is the advantage of just measuring the
probabilities.  If the sample is large enough (and 1000 is already a
pretty decent size, if you don't have too many that ended prematurely
among them :-) ), you will get a decent average automatically including
all these differences.  If you wanted to calculate them from the
"algorithm of the game" you would have to go to painstaking efforts,
which I am not prepared to take at this moment.


